# Independent HTML-budget documentation delta review

Fixed commit 4de46baf5f22f8dcb5e99d0312d9b61e36a44d6b. Reviewed root-authored new specification/draft paragraph and the three development-guide lines; no edits to production, source specification or manuscripts. The reviewer authored earlier chapter 16 text but not this root-authored delta. This is an independent delta review, not a renewed independent whole-chapter semantic review.

No blocking discrepancy found. The exact eight source sentences and draft visible base text agree, including code-adjacent spaces, output separation, all eight resources, omitted defaults, null/partial/unknown/negative rejection, selection before entry, unchanged separate parse/lower operations, no stopped retry or Markdown-budget fallback, no output directory on pre-write stop, execution provenance and the raw-registry effect on new Markdown input context. The two InlineCode values are exactly html_output_limits and output_limits, in order. No links, table cells, translations or code blocks are added or altered.

All existing draft bytes are preserved: independent line diff finds one ten-line insertion, and removing it reconstructs the entire parent Git blob. Full article formal structure passes. The new paragraph contains eight Sentence nodes; two are explicit prefix Sentence/InlineCode and six are ordinary sentence literals, matching authoring.md. All 49 Ruby bases are Kanji-only; ordinary added Text contains no unannotated Kanji. Readings were directly reviewed, including 集合書出/しゅうごうかきだ + し, 指定時/していじ, 負数/ふすう, 一/ひと + つ, 書込/かきこ + み, and 開始時/かいしじ. No non-Kanji was included in the Ruby bases.

Code correspondence was read, not inferred from tests: canonical.rs Registry uses serde default on a concrete OutputLimits field and deny_unknown_fields; OutputLimits aliases the existing eight required u64 OperationLimits with its existing Doc-host default. canonical::html creates exactly the selected HTML output Budget before generation, calls generate_with_output_budget, and only writes after successful generation. It does not borrow the Markdown output_limits. The existing page generator uses default PhaseLimits for distinct per-page parse/lower operations, then passes the shared output Budget through PageSet rendering and serialization. Its existing manifest records selected limits, initial usage and execution_identity; no new semantic Document identity is invented. canonical/projection.rs retains original raw registry bytes in the length-framed context, so the HTML-only field still changes new-profile Markdown metadata. The new development lines accurately describe explicit finite selection and a separately recorded new invocation, not a hidden retry within a stopped operation.

Nonblocking draft synchronization note: doc/migration/authored/guide/development.nepld does not yet contain the new html_output_limits explanation. This requested delta updates its canonical Markdown only; the existing guide draft should receive that three-line addition in a later explicit authoring task before its own canonical cutover. No guide draft was modified during this review.

Executed: check.py formal structure and independent source/display/code/Ruby/byte-preservation assertions; git diff --check for the three reviewed documentation files. No native/WASI production parse/lower/labels, output generation, resource-fault execution or browser testing was run by this reviewer. Root's pending/full canonical tests are separate evidence and are not converted into independent passes here.

Probe correction retained in scope: initial isolated insertion parsing assumed difflib would include the opening cons paragraph. Its valid insertion alignment instead started at the first Sentence and ended after the next identical paragraph marker, causing the scratch assertion to reject cons as a Block. The final checker parses the full article and selects the actual paragraph by its unique html_output_limits code node, while preserving the independent whole-byte insertion proof. This was a scratch extraction issue, not a draft or runtime failure.
